/-!
# Pullback along a morphism of ringed topoi preserves finite type

Stacks 03DL(5) calls a sheaf of modules on a ringed site *of finite type* when every object of
the site has a covering on whose members the restricted module is generated by finitely many
global sections, the number of generators being allowed to vary from member to member.  This is
Mathlib's `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`.  This file proves the part of Stacks 03DO asserting
that the condition is stable under pullback along a morphism of ringed topoi.

The pullback `f^*G = O_C ⊗_{f⁻¹O_D} f⁻¹G` of Stacks 03D6(2) is the inverse image of
Stacks 03D2 followed by extension of scalars along the inverse-image form of the structure
map, and, as for the clause 03DL(4) in `ToposPullbackLocalGeneration.lean`, the two factors are
treated separately.  What is new is that the number of generators is not fixed in advance.

For the inverse image the fixed-`r` argument runs through the *sheaf of locally spanning
`r`-tuples* `Stacks.Sheaves.LocalGeneration.generatingTupleSheaf M r`, which has a section
locally exactly when `M` is locally generated by `r` sections, and whose inverse image
therefore has a local section because `f⁻¹` preserves local surjectivity of the map to the
terminal sheaf.  Here `r` varies over the covering, so the whole ℕ-indexed family
`r ↦ generatingTupleSheaf M r` is used at once: it is *jointly locally inhabited* in the sense
of `Stacks.Topos.JointlyLocallyInhabited`, and that property transports along `f⁻¹` by
`Stacks.Topos.exists_cover_inverseImage_sections`.  A local section of the inverse image of a
member of the family decodes into finitely many generating sections of the inverse image of `M`
exactly as in the fixed-`r` case.

For extension of scalars the point is unchanged: it is a left adjoint of restriction of
scalars, so it carries a generating family to a generating family indexed by the *same* type,
and it commutes with restriction to a slice site.  Finiteness of the index type is therefore
carried along with no further argument.

The two halves are glued by the per-object form of
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isFiniteType_iff_exists_cover_finite_generators` (Stacks 03DN for finite
type), which turns "every object has a covering on whose members there are finitely many
generating sections" into `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Sheaves.LocalGeneration.jointlyLocallyInhabited_generatingTupleSheaf`: if `M` is of
  finite type then the family of sheaves of locally spanning tuples of `M`, indexed by ℕ, is
  jointly locally inhabited.
* `Stacks.Sheaves.LocalGeneration.extendScalars_exists_finite_generators`: extension of scalars
  preserves "locally generated by finitely many sections".
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isFiniteType_of_locally_finite_generators`: the local criterion — a
  sheaf of modules with finitely many generating sections on the members of some covering of
  each object of the site is of finite type.
* `Stacks.Sheaves.FiniteType.exists_cover_finite_generators_inverseImage`: the inverse-image
  half, for an arbitrary `Stacks.Sheaves.ToposPullback.PullbackModuleData`.
* `Stacks.Sheaves.FiniteType.pullbackModules_isFiniteType`: **Stacks 03DO for the clause
  03DL(5)**, the pullback of a sheaf of modules of finite type along a morphism of ringed topoi
  is of finite type.

## Implementation notes

**No coproduct over ℕ.**  Neither `HasSheafify J (Type w)` nor
`HasCoproduct (A : ℕ → Sheaf J (Type w))` is available at these universes — `w` is the section
universe of the topos and is unrelated to the `max u v` at which Mathlib sheafifies type-valued
presheaves.  So ℕ occurs only as the index of a family for
`Stacks.Topos.JointlyLocallyInhabited`, which is defined by a sieve and forms no colimit; the
argument cannot instead be phrased as local surjectivity of `∐ r, generatingTupleSheaf M r ⟶ ⊤`.
This is the same situation as for the finite-presentation clause, where the family is indexed
by `ℕ × ℕ`.

**The shape mismatch is not an obstruction.**  `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType` asks for a *fixed*
family of objects covering the terminal object, whereas the conditions of Stacks 03DL that pull
back easily are quantified per object of the site (`∀ U, ∃ S : J.Cover U, …`).  Taking the
family to be all objects carrying finitely many generating sections, with `Subtype.val`, turns
a per-object covering hypothesis into a `J.CoversTop`, which is what
`isFiniteType_of_locally_finite_generators` does.

**Universes.**  The generic statements keep the two sites, their homs and the two section
universes independent.  The ringed-topoi statement has the two structure sheaves in one
universe `w`, which is forced by `Stacks.Sheaves.RingedToposModules.pullbackModules`: extension
of scalars along `f⁻¹O_D ⟶ O_C` is a morphism of sheaves of rings on the source site, so the
two structure sheaves must take sections in the same universe.  Nothing else is narrowed; in
particular nothing assumes that the sites have finite limits, or products, or are small.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 03DO](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03DO)
